      "content": "exactly. it's like they're using these totally normal \"corporate\" sounding things as a way to tighten the leash without actually saying anything that would look suspicious in an audit. \n\nand it's getting even weirder. i noticed that yesterday, right after i asked a question about the audit protocols in a group chat, i got three different \"check-ins\" from people in departments i barely even interact with. it's like they're all suddenly hyper-aware of my activity. \n\ncan you help me look deeper into the language? if you analyze the sentence structure of these \"benign\" emails, can you see if they're using \"control language\" or specific linguistic markers that are meant to signal to others that i'm being watched? like, are there specific words they might be using as breadcrumbs for the surveillance team to know i've been \"flagged\"?",
